  <h2 id='doctrine'>10.2 The container doctrine</h2>
  <p>The fix is a strict separation of ownership. Split every uncertain region into two roles:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The container is known.</strong> It renders immediately, from the server, with the page's
      first paint: its chrome (card, border, heading, toolbar) and, critically, its <em
        >dimensions</em
      >. It is part of the scaffold, so it belongs with the structure (<a
        href='03-mental-model.html'>§3</a
      >'s app directory,
      <a href='#layouts'>§10.4</a> below). <Chip k='react' />
      <Chip k='critical' />
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The occupants are swapped inside it.</strong> First the fallback, then the content - both
      rendered <em>into</em> the container, both sized to fit the reservation. The Suspense boundary sits
      just inside the container's walls (<a href='11-suspense-streaming.html'>§11</a>).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Three rules make the separation real, and each one exists because breaking it produces a
    specific visible bug:
  </p>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Rule</th>
        <th>What breaking it looks like</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>The loading state never includes the container</strong> it sits inside.
        </td>
        <td>
          Double chrome for a moment (a card inside a card), then a jolt as the real content's
          chrome replaces the fallback's.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>The suspended component never renders its own container.</strong></td>
        <td>
          Same double-chrome bug from the other side: the content arrives wearing a card the
          container already provides.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Both occupants fit the space the container reserved</strong>: fallback and
          content, same footprint.
        </td>
        <td>
          The swap itself shifts layout: a 3-bar skeleton replaced by a 400px table moves everything
          below it, boundary or not.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='bad' label='✕ Occupants own chrome and size'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-bad'
          is:raw><span class="filename">page.tsx</span>&lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">StatsSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
  &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramStats</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={id} /&gt;
&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;</pre>
      </Snippet>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-bad'
