  <p>
    Two more shift sources worth closing here. <strong>Fonts</strong>: a late-swapping
    webfont reflows every line of text; <code>next/font</code> preloads and applies size-adjusted fallbacks
    so text renders in its final metrics from the start
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' />. And
    <strong>conditionally-injected UI</strong> (banners, alerts, toolbars that appear after a check):
    give them reserved slots or overlay them; never insert them into the flow above existing content.
  </p>

  <h2 id='layouts'>10.4 Containers live in the scaffold</h2>
  <p>
    Where should containers be rendered? As high as their stability allows, which usually means
    <strong>in the layout or page, as part of the app directory's scaffold</strong> (<a
      href='03-mental-model.html'>§3</a
    >). <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' /> The payoff is navigation: layouts persist across route changes; they don't re-render
    when you move between sibling pages (<a href='03-mental-model.html#rendering-model'>§3.1</a>). A
    container rendered in the layout is therefore stable not just against its own content loading,
    but against
    <em>navigation itself</em>: moving from one program's report to another swaps what's inside the
    containers while the frame (header, tabs, table chrome) never repaints. The page stops feeling
    like a sequence of pages and starts feeling like an application.
  </p>

